<title>Hyperprolactinemia: Management.</title>
<graphic href="eee0138-w0001.gif" />
<cell type="start" id="cell-1">
<label>Hyperprolactinemia. Pregnant?</label>
<path target="cell-3">Yes</path>
<path target="cell-2">No</path>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-2">
<label>TSH level?</label>
<path target="cell-4">TSH elevated</path>
<path target="cell-5">TSH normal</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-4">
<label>If prolactin &gt;50 ng&#x002F;mL or pituitary tumor suspected clinically, order MRI and consider medications.</label>
<path target="cell-6"></path>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-6">
<label>Replace thyroxine and repeat prolactin.</label>
<path target="cell-7">Normal</path>
<path target="cell-8">Elevated</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-7">
<label>No further work-up.</label>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-5">
<label>Drug-related hyperprolactinemia? (See Table 1.)</label>
<path target="cell-9">Yes</path>
<path target="cell-8">No</path>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-8">
<label>Order MRI of pituitary. Result?</label>
<path target="cell-10">No pathology</path>
<path target="cell-11">Tumor</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-10">
<label>Treat the hyperprolactinemia, if indicated, with medications (see text).</label>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-11">
<label>Treat this pathology as appropriate (medical or surgical).</label>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-9">
<label>Drug-related hyperprolactinemia.</label>
<path target="cell-12"></path>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-12">
<label>Prolactin &gt;50 ng/mL?</label>
<path target="cell-8">Yes</path>
<path target="cell-13">No</path>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-13">
<label>Attempt withdrawal of offending drug. Withdrawal not possible or prolactin still elevated?</label>
<path target="cell-8">Yes</path>
<path target="cell-14">No (normal prolactin level)</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-14">
<label>No further work-up.</label>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-3">
<label>If no other cause of hyperprolactinemia is suspected, no further work-up.</label>
</cell>
</algorithm>
